---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import lectures from '../content/lectures/lectures.json';
import { BASE } from '../lib/constants';

const lectureCount = lectures.length;
---

<Base title="Introduction to AI in Medicine Course Materials">

  <!-- Hero section -->
  <section class="section-band">
    <div class="content-col reveal-on-scroll">
      <p class="section-label">Open-Source Course Materials</p>
      <h1>Introduction to<br/>AI in Medicine</h1>
      <p class="mt-8 text-lg md:text-xl text-ink-secondary max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        A course designed to introduce medical students with zero prior experience to foundational concepts,
        tools, and applications of artificial intelligence in clinical and biomedical contexts.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-12 flex flex-wrap gap-6">
        <a href={`${BASE}/syllabus`} class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 bg-ink text-paper px-6 py-3 rounded-full text-[15px] font-medium no-underline hover:bg-ink/80 transition-colors">
          View Syllabus
        </a>
        <a
          href="https://github.com/slostett/Intro_to_AI_in_Medicine_Course_Materials"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 border border-rule text-ink px-6 py-3 rounded-full text-[15px] font-medium no-underline hover:border-ink transition-colors"
        >
          GitHub Repository ↗
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Background -->
  <section class="section-band-alt">
    <div class="content-col reveal-on-scroll">
      <p class="section-label">Background</p>
      <h2>Why this course exists</h2>
      <div class="mt-10 max-w-2xl space-y-6 text-[17px] text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          FDA-cleared AI and machine learning medical devices grew from 29 in 2017 to more than 690 by 2023, and 66%
          of physicians reported using health AI in 2024, up from 38% just a year earlier. Medical school curricula
          have not kept pace: most existing AI content in undergraduate medical education raises awareness of these
          tools without building the technical competency needed to critically evaluate them.
        </p>
        <p>
          The educational options that do go deeper are largely out of reach. Executive certificate programs at
          places like Harvard Medical School and MIT Sloan cost thousands of dollars and sit outside the required
          curriculum, and students at community-based or resource-limited programs often have far fewer
          opportunities than those at large research institutions. That leaves most students choosing between
          expensive extracurricular training and cursory in-house coverage, neither of which prepares them to
          critically evaluate, safely implement, or help build the AI systems they will encounter in practice.
        </p>
        <p>
          This course was built to close that gap: a free, open-source curriculum that starts from first
          principles and requires nothing beyond standard premedical math.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- At a glance -->
  <section class="section-band">
    <div class="content-col reveal-on-scroll">
      <p class="section-label">At a Glance</p>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-12 md:gap-8 mt-12">
        <div>
          <p class="text-3xl font-semibold text-ink tabular-nums tracking-tighter">{lectureCount}</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] text-ink-secondary">Lectures covering the full landscape of AI in medicine</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="text-3xl font-semibold text-ink tabular-nums tracking-tighter">4</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] text-ink-secondary">Pillars of AI: data, loss, network, and training</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="text-3xl font-semibold text-ink tabular-nums tracking-tighter">1</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] text-ink-secondary">Final project with real clinical questions</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Pedagogical approach -->
  <section class="section-band-alt">
    <div class="content-col reveal-on-scroll">
      <p class="section-label">Approach</p>
      <h2>Build the technical understanding<br/>to be a leader in AI</h2>
      <div class="mt-10 max-w-2xl space-y-6 text-[17px] text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          This course bridges the mathematical fundementals of AI with clinical practice with a new framework: the Four Pillars Model. 
          By focusing on key concepts that link mathematics and real world applications, students understand how changes made at an architectural level have applications in the clinic.
          This approach gives you the foundation you need to make change, critically appraise pre-packaged industry solutions and design your own interventions, not simply memorize bulletpoints.
        </p>
        <p>
          The first six lectures make up the foundation module, where you'll learn to bridge the mathematical underpinnings of AI with real decision making.
          The remaining lectures form the application module, where you'll use what you learned to understand cutting edge technologies, critiquing and changing them to fit your needs.
        </p>
        <p>
          The course includes interactive demos, clinical case studies, guest lectures from practitioners (not included on this site),
          and a final project where you explore a clinically relevant question using AI methods. Several example final projects by real students are available for review.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Pillars diagram -->
  <section class="section-band">
    <div class="content-col reveal-on-scroll">
      <p class="section-label">Framework</p>
      <h2>Four Pillars of AI in Medicine</h2>
      <p class="mt-6 text-[17px] text-ink-secondary max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        A high-level conceptual diagram outlining the core components required for building and evaluating
        AI systems in medical contexts: data, loss, network, and training. Throughout the course, students were asked to use and complete this template to evaluate and critique AI through the lens of the four pillars.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-10 flex flex-wrap gap-4">
        <a
          href={`${BASE}/4%20pillars%20diagram%20empty.pdf`}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 border border-rule text-ink px-5 py-2.5 rounded-full text-[15px] no-underline hover:border-ink transition-colors"
        >
          Diagram (Blank) ↗
        </a>
        <a
          href={`${BASE}/4%20pillars%20diagram%20filled%20out.pdf`}
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 border border-rule text-ink px-5 py-2.5 rounded-full text-[15px] no-underline hover:border-ink transition-colors"
        >
          Diagram (Filled) ↗
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Methodology -->
  <section class="section-band-alt">
    <div class="content-col reveal-on-scroll">
      <p class="section-label">Methodology</p>
      <h2>How the pilot was run</h2>
      <div class="mt-10 max-w-2xl space-y-6 text-[17px] text-ink-secondary leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          The course was piloted as a 20-session, one-credit elective for fourth-year medical students at the
          University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill School of Medicine in Fall 2025, meeting twice weekly for an
          hour in the evening from September to November so that students on rotations or away could still attend.
          Twenty students enrolled for credit, with several others auditing.
        </p>
        <p>
          Guest lecturers included UNC Hospitals' Director of Cardiovascular Artificial Intelligence Research, a
          venture capital expert from the Wharton School, and a clinical AI developer from Doximity. One session
          was run as an AI rollout simulation, where students rotated through stations with a practicing clinician,
          an industry AI developer, and a standardized patient, practicing how to raise and respond to concerns
          about a new AI tool from each stakeholder's perspective.
        </p>
        <p>
          To measure the course's effect, students completed an anonymous Likert-scale survey before and after the
          course, covering eight items on AI knowledge, confidence, and attitudes toward bias and literature
          evaluation. Because the pre- and post-course groups were small and unequal in size (9 and 13
          respondents), Mann-Whitney U tests were used to assess whether the observed shifts were statistically
          significant.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Survey outcomes -->
  <section class="section-band">
    <div class="content-col reveal-on-scroll">
      <p class="section-label">Survey Outcomes</p>
      <h2>Measurable gains in confidence</h2>
      <p class="mt-6 text-[17px] text-ink-secondary max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        All eight survey items improved from pre- to post-course, and every improvement was statistically
        significant (p &lt; 0.05).
      </p>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-12 md:gap-8 mt-12">
        <div>
          <p class="text-3xl font-semibold text-ink tabular-nums tracking-tighter">90.9%</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] text-ink-secondary">
            of students agreed or strongly agreed they could answer basic AI questions from colleagues, up from a
            baseline where 88% were neutral, disagreed, or strongly disagreed
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="text-3xl font-semibold text-ink tabular-nums tracking-tighter">81%</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] text-ink-secondary">
            reported feeling comfortable critically reading medical AI literature after the course
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="text-3xl font-semibold text-ink tabular-nums tracking-tighter">80%</p>
          <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] text-ink-secondary">
            positive shift in comfort investigating bias in AI systems, despite the course's mathematical rigor
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-16 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-[15px] border-collapse min-w-[640px]">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-rule">
              <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-medium text-ink-secondary">Survey Item</th>
              <th class="py-3 px-4 font-medium text-ink-secondary text-center">Pre Median</th>
              <th class="py-3 px-4 font-medium text-ink-secondary text-center">Post Median</th>
              <th class="py-3 pl-4 font-medium text-ink-secondary text-center">p-value</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="border-b border-rule">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-ink-secondary">I know what AI is</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-center tabular-nums text-ink">4</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-center tabular-nums text-ink">4</td>
              <td class="py-3 pl-4 text-center tabular-nums text-ink">0.029</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-rule">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-ink-secondary">
                When a new AI technology comes out, I feel like I have the knowledge to understand how it works
                myself
              </td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-center tabular-nums text-ink">3</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-center tabular-nums text-ink">4</td>
              <td class="py-3 pl-4 text-center tabular-nums text-ink">0.002</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-rule">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-ink-secondary">
                Knowing the math behind AI is helpful to know how a given AI system works
              </td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-center tabular-nums text-ink">3</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-center tabular-nums text-ink">4</td>
              <td class="py-3 pl-4 text-center tabular-nums text-ink">0.029</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-rule">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-ink-secondary">
                I feel like I could answer basic questions about AI if asked by my medical colleagues
              </td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-center tabular-nums text-ink">3</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-center tabular-nums text-ink">4</td>
              <td class="py-3 pl-4 text-center tabular-nums text-ink">0.003</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-rule">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-ink-secondary">
                I feel like I could answer basic questions about AI if asked by computer scientists
              </td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-center tabular-nums text-ink">2</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-center tabular-nums text-ink">3</td>
              <td class="py-3 pl-4 text-center tabular-nums text-ink">0.003</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-rule">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-ink-secondary">
                I feel like I could lead a team to develop my own AI intervention if I had an idea for one
              </td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-center tabular-nums text-ink">1</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-center tabular-nums text-ink">4</td>
              <td class="py-3 pl-4 text-center tabular-nums text-ink">&lt;0.001</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-rule">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-ink-secondary">
                I feel comfortable investigating how AI systems might be biased toward certain outcomes
              </td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-center tabular-nums text-ink">2</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-center tabular-nums text-ink">4</td>
              <td class="py-3 pl-4 text-center tabular-nums text-ink">&lt;0.001</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-rule">
              <td class="py-3 pr-4 text-ink-secondary">I feel comfortable critically reading medical AI literature</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-center tabular-nums text-ink">3</td>
              <td class="py-3 px-4 text-center tabular-nums text-ink">4</td>
              <td class="py-3 pl-4 text-center tabular-nums text-ink">0.009</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-ink-secondary">
          Median Likert scores (1–5 scale), pre-course n=9, post-course n=13. Significance assessed with the
          Mann-Whitney U test.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-10 text-[17px] text-ink-secondary max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        This was a pilot with a small number of survey respondents, and the course will continue to be taught and
        refined in future semesters with a larger cohort.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

</Base>
